{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Eightfold.DualityIntertwinerIsHermitianForm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Eightfold.DualityIntertwinerIsHermitianForm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.hermitian_representative","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.hermitian_representative","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DualityIntertwinerIsHermitianForm.lean#L103-L114","doc":"**STEP 2, assembled.**  From an intertwiner whose `dagger` is a scalar multiple\nof itself, and an involutive `sigma`, a Hermitian representative is produced.\n\nThe norm-one condition `sigma c * c = 1` is not assumed here -- it is DERIVED in the\nprobe by applying `dagger` twice -- but it is what makes the Hilbert-90 witness\navailable, so it appears as a hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.hermitian_of_hilbert90_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.hermitian_of_hilbert90_witness","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DualityIntertwinerIsHermitianForm.lean#L75-L101","doc":"**STEP 2b — the Hermitian normalisation.**\n\nWrite `A† := (Aᵀ).map sigma`.  The form `H(v,w) = vᵀ A sigma(w)` is sigma-Hermitian\nexactly when `A† = A`.\n\nSchur gives `A† = c • A` for a scalar `c` (the invariant form is unique up to a\nscalar, so `A†`, being invariant too, is a multiple of `A`).  Given any `d` with\n`sigma d * c = d` -- which `hilbert90_witness` supplies -- the rescaled matrix\n`d • A` is Hermitian on the nose.\n\nNo hypothesis that `d ≠ 0`: the identity holds regardless, and it is the caller's\njob to pick an `x` making the witness nonzero.  That is honest about where the work\nis: existence of a NONDEGENERATE Hermitian representative needs `d ≠ 0`, which is a\ngenericity statement, not an identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.hilbert90_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.hilbert90_witness","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DualityIntertwinerIsHermitianForm.lean#L54-L73","doc":"**STEP 2a — Hilbert 90, constructively, for a norm-one scalar.**\n\nIf `sigma` is an involution and `N(c) = sigma c * c = 1`, then `c` is a ratio\n`d / sigma d`, and a witness `d` is produced rather than merely asserted to exist:\n`d := x + c * sigma x` works for every `x`.\n\nThis is the classical Hilbert-90 argument for a degree-2 extension, and it is the\nstep that makes the Hermitian normalisation below CONSTRUCTIVE.  Note the conclusion\nis stated as `sigma d * c = d`, which is the form the matrix lemma consumes, and\nwhich avoids having to know `d ≠ 0` to state it.  Nondegeneracy of `d` is a separate\nmatter and is exactly what the caller must check. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.invariantForm_of_intertwiner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.invariantForm_of_intertwiner","kind":"theorem","line":30,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/DualityIntertwinerIsHermitianForm.lean#L30-L52","doc":"**STEP 1 — the intertwiner relation is an invariant-form relation.**\n\nThe duality datum is an invertible `A` with `A * sigma(M) * A⁻¹ = (Mᵀ)⁻¹` for every\ngroup element `M` in the image of the representation.  Written without the inverse of\n`A`, and with `Minv` standing for `(Mᵀ)⁻¹`, that is `A * sigma(M) = Minv * A`.\n\nThis lemma is the one line of rearrangement that turns it into\n`Mᵀ * A * sigma(M) = A`, i.e. into the statement that the sesquilinear form\n`H(v, w) = vᵀ A sigma(w)` is invariant under `v, w ↦ M v, M w`.\n\nNo hypothesis on `sigma` at all -- not even additivity is used beyond its being a\nring hom, and involutivity is NOT needed here.  The content is entirely that\n`Mᵀ * Minv = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}